WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.920
Enterprise mobile applications demand a robust architecture capable of handling everything

2
00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:11.000
from lightweight user preferences to heavily encrypted session tokens.

3
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:16.840
In native iOS development, this storage hierarchy is strictly categorized into four foundational

4
00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:18.240
pillars.

5
00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:24.680
As this diagram illustrates on the left, we have user defaults, NS file manager, core data,

6
00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:26.080
and the keychain.

7
00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:30.200
The right side, representing React Native, is currently blank.

8
00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:34.840
When engineers transition to React Native, they often make a critical error.

9
00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:40.120
They treat JavaScript storage as a generic web abstraction instead of a strict interface

10
00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:42.280
for these native layers.

11
00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:47.760
Defaulting to React Native's built-in async storage creates an immediate bottleneck.

12
00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:51.880
Everything running through it narrows into a slow asynchronous crawl.

13
00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:56.120
My performance architecture requires abandoning that generic approach.

14
00:00:56.120 --> 00:01:01.400
You must map each native iOS storage layer to a purpose built JavaScript wrapper.

15
00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:07.960
These specific wrappers bridge directly to the underlying C and objective C APIs, bypassing

16
00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:11.360
the standard React Native bottlenecks entirely.

17
00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:16.360
By aligning the JavaScript interface with these specific layers, the application can maintain

18
00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:22.240
synchronous execution, hardware level encryption, and the exact system semantics defined by

19
00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:23.560
iOS.

20
00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:28.040
We start at the highest, most lightweight layer of the storage hierarchy.

21
00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:31.520
User preferences, feature flags, and session tokens.

22
00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:37.320
On iOS, these values go into user defaults, which is a synchronous key value store backed

23
00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:39.600
by a simple pleased file.

24
00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:43.240
React Native developers traditionally reach for asynchronous here.

25
00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:49.320
The flaw in this approach is that asynchronous relies on asynchronous, slow IO operations.

26
00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:54.920
When we test this against a native synchronous store, the performance deficit becomes obvious.

27
00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:58.400
This horizontal bar chart compares execution times.

28
00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:03.560
The top bar shows an asynchronous read and write taking 2 to 5 milliseconds.

29
00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:11.520
The bottom bar, React Native MMKV, executes at a fraction, 0.1 to 0.3 milliseconds.

30
00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:16.240
It locks into place as the exact architectural equivalent to user data.

31
00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:22.600
For the most ergonomic implementation, developers persist a zoo stand state store using MMKV as

32
00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:28.400
the back end, perfectly mirroring the behavior of the app storage wrapper in SwiftUI.

33
00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:33.240
Shifting preference storage back to the synchronous thread removes the IO bottleneck from the UI

34
00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:38.640
loop, allowing key value data to be available immediately on mount, without managing loading

35
00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:41.400
states or asynchronous lag.

36
00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:46.880
Starting up from user preferences, we have large binary data and user generated files.

37
00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:52.840
Expo file system interfaces with the native iOS, NS file manager, breaking down into three

38
00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:54.840
sandbox environments.

39
00:02:54.840 --> 00:03:00.600
The document directory persists across updates for user generated content and iCloud backups.

40
00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:05.320
The cache directory carries no backup and is actively targeted for deletion during device

41
00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:06.560
storage pressure.

42
00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:12.480
Finally, the temporary directory is completely wiped every time the device reboots.

43
00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:16.280
Placing files in the wrong directory has severe consequences.

44
00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:21.360
Dumping cache files into the document directory causes immediate iCloud backup bloat, while

45
00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:26.480
placing user documents in the cache ensures those assets will be permanently deleted during

46
00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:28.120
OS sweeps.

47
00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:33.280
The next layer handles complex object graphs and structured relational data.

48
00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:38.000
SwiftNative lacks a direct framework level equivalent to Apple's core data or Swift

49
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:39.600
data paradigms.

50
00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:45.040
In native development, SwiftData's query macro automatically pushes database changes directly

51
00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:50.400
to the SwiftUI view layer, keeping the interface instantly synchronized with the data.

52
00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:55.680
We build the foundation for this in React Native using Expo SQLite version 2, which provides

53
00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:58.480
modern asynchronous SQLite operations.

54
00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:02.000
However, sqelite alone is just a database.

55
00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:07.080
It lacks the auto-updating reactivity that iOS developers expect out of the box.

56
00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:10.440
We patch this reactivity gap using TANSTAC query.

57
00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:13.840
This continuous looping logic flow illustrates the pattern.

58
00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:19.000
The Expo sqelite database feeds data up into TANSTAC query, which then handles the caching

59
00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:23.200
layer and pushes real-time updates directly into the UI component.

60
00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:28.440
By pairing use query for read operations and invalidate queries for rights, we build a fully

61
00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:30.760
synchronized UI state cache.

62
00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:35.560
This stack effectively replicates the reactive data flow of Swift data, ensuring that structural

63
00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:40.200
changes in the sqelite database are automatically reflected in the react native UI through

64
00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:41.600
the query cache.

65
00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:44.960
The final layer of our architecture is the most restricted.

66
00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:50.080
This is where we handle highly sensitive secrets, API keys, and cryptographic tokens.

67
00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:54.920
The iOS keychain is physically protected by the device's secure enclave and tied directly

68
00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:57.120
to the user's passcode.

69
00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:04.440
To access this in react native, we use Expo secure store, which wraps the native CAPIs

70
00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:07.880
used to add and copy keychain items.

71
00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:14.680
By default, this wrapper routes to the accessible when unlocked iOS protection class, ensuring

72
00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:19.240
data is only available while the device is actively in use.

73
00:05:19.240 --> 00:05:26.040
For elevated security, developers can explicitly enforce Face ID or Touch ID hardware checks

74
00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:31.040
by enabling the require authentication flag in their configuration.

75
00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:34.760
There is a strict physical constraint to keep in mind here.

76
00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:38.880
The keychain is engineered exclusively for tiny payloads.

77
00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:44.560
If you attempt to store large data sets in the secure enclave, the operation will fail.

78
00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:48.200
Securing large files requires a split architectural pattern.

79
00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:53.240
You generate an encryption key, store only that key inside the secure store, and place

80
00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:57.600
the heavily encrypted large file out in the document directory.

81
00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:02.320
Treating the secure store as a standard database triggers memory limits and degrade system

82
00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:03.320
performance.

83
00:06:03.320 --> 00:06:08.280
It must be utilized strictly as a cryptographic key management system to maintain the integrity

84
00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:13.000
of the enclave, implementing the structure requires special care during a brownfield

85
00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:18.640
integration, where an existing native iOS application is migrating to react native.

86
00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:22.480
The immediate danger during this transition is trapped user data.

87
00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:27.040
With the legacy native stores remain untouched, you end up with a split brain architecture where

88
00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:31.200
the old app and new app rely on entirely different sources of truth.

89
00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:35.840
To resolve this, we execute a one-time migration sequence during the application's startup

90
00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:37.080
phase.

91
00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:40.400
This flow chart illustrates the custom turbo-module process.

92
00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:45.560
First, the turbo-module extracts the known service names, accounts, and preferences directly

93
00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:47.400
from the legacy native stores.

94
00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:52.240
Second, we immediately re-white this extracted data into our newly established react native

95
00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:56.960
wrappers, passing it to react native MMKV and expo secure store.

96
00:06:56.960 --> 00:07:02.000
Finally, we execute a hard deletion of the original native records, ensuring the react

97
00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:05.240
native wrappers become the sole source of truth.

98
00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:10.800
Returning to our architectural mapping from earlier, the dual column diagram is now complete.

99
00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:17.040
User data is perfectly aligned to MMKV, NS file manager maps to file system, core data

100
00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:23.000
pairs with SQLite and TANstack query, and the keychain maps directly to the secure store.

101
00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:27.600
The layer boundaries that iOS enforces at the hardware and operating system level are

102
00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:31.160
the exact boundaries react native developers must respect.

103
00:07:31.160 --> 00:07:36.480
By strictly aligning your data payload characteristics with the precise native wrapper, a JavaScript-driven

104
00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:41.800
framework achieves the exact security, scalability, and synchronous performance of pure Swift code.

